  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "/8TSQL12y68cu669ReUktDcQ2ow=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:55:10.313Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Every `IAnalyticsStore` implementation \u2014 `KnexAnalyticsStore`, `PostgresAnalyticsStore`, `MemoryAnalyticsStore`, `BrowserAnalyticsStore` \u2014 takes two optional logging hooks in its constructor options:\n\n```ts\nconst queryLogger   = (index, query)   => console.log(`[Q:${index}] ${query}`);\nconst resultsLogger = (index, results) => console.log(`[R:${index}] ${JSON.stringify(results)}`);\nconst store = new MemoryAnalyticsStore({ queryLogger, resultsLogger });\n```\n\nThe detail that makes them useful rather than noisy: **\"since queries are asynchronous operations, indices match between query and results functions.\"** So a log line can be paired with its own result even when several queries are in flight \u2014 which is exactly the case where an unindexed log is useless.\n\n`defaultQueryLogger(tag)` and `defaultResultsLogger(tag)` are the included implementations, appending a tag to each line so two stores in one process stay distinguishable.\n\nFor measurement rather than debugging there is `IAnalyticsProfiler`, consumed by each store, with a default `AnalyticsProfiler` in `@powerhousedao/analytics-engine-core` and a `PassthroughAnalyticsProfiler` for when profiling should be inert.\n\nWhy this matters beyond convenience: these stores generate SQL from typed TypeScript objects, so the logger is the only way to see what a dimensional query actually became \u2014 which is where an unexpectedly slow rollup usually explains itself.\n\n## Provenance\n- `apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/04-WorkWithData/_06-Analytics Engine/typescript/utilities.md` \u2014 SqlQueryLogger and SqlResultsLogger; IAnalyticsProfiler\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:55:10.313Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:55:10.313Z"
    }
  },
